# Expense Reimbursement Policy — submission and time limits

> Module ClaimWindow

E-6 is a three-step staircase on one quantity: the number of days between the
date the expense was incurred and the date the claim was submitted. All three
clauses define the same variable, `admissible`, in one exception chain, because
E-6.2 is expressly "by way of exception to E-6.1" and E-6.3 bounds E-6.2.

## Prologue — declarations

```catala-metadata
declaration scope ClaimAdmissible:
  input expense_incurred_date content date
  input submission_date content date
  input delay_certified_for_good_reason content boolean
  output days_elapsed content integer
  output admissible content boolean
```

`delay_certified_for_good_reason` is the **judgement input** for this module.
E-6.2 is HYBRID: the 60/120-day window is exact arithmetic, but "good reason"
has no test in the Policy, and the thing that triggers the exception is the line
manager's certification of it. That certification is supplied to the scope. The
module does not, and must not, infer good reason from the length of the delay,
from the amount claimed, or from anything else.

## E-6.1 The 60-day window

| EXP-POL E-6.1 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:94)
|
| A claim must be submitted within 60 days after the date on which the
| expense was incurred.

The elapsed period runs from the date the expense was incurred, so a claim
submitted on the day the expense was incurred has elapsed nothing. "Within 60
days" includes the sixtieth day: day 60 is admissible on this limb and day 61 is
not.

The scope asserts that the claim was not submitted before the expense was
incurred, because a negative elapsed period is not a fact E-6 contemplates and
silently admitting it would make the staircase meaningless.

```catala
scope ClaimAdmissible:
  definition days_elapsed equals
    integer of ((submission_date - expense_incurred_date) / (1 day))

  label e6_1 definition admissible equals days_elapsed <= 60

  assertion submission_date >= expense_incurred_date
```

## E-6.2 Late claim certified for good reason

| EXP-POL E-6.2 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:97)
|
| By way of exception to E-6.1, a claim submitted after 60 days but
| within 120 days may be reimbursed where the Employee's line manager certifies
| that the delay was for good reason.

An exception to `e6_1`, and the window it carries is its own: "after 60 days
but within 120 days" is days 61 to 120 inclusive. Both bounds are stated in the
condition rather than inherited — a Catala exception does not inherit its
parent's condition, and here the parent's condition (`days_elapsed <= 60`) is
not one that may hold at all: E-6.2 governs precisely the days on which E-6.1's
own test fails, which is why the window is restated as `> 60 and <= 120`.

E-6.2 is permissive — "may be reimbursed" — and this scope answers the question
E-6 asks, which is whether the claim is admissible. With the certification in
hand it is; whether the Company then exercises a discretion to refuse is not a
second gate this module invents, and the permissive wording is carried in the
vector store as prose against this clause.

```catala
scope ClaimAdmissible:
  label e6_2 exception e6_1 definition admissible
    under condition
      days_elapsed > 60
      and days_elapsed < 120
      and delay_certified_for_good_reason
    consequence equals true
```

## E-6.3 The 120-day long-stop

| EXP-POL E-6.3 (002-expense-reimbursement-policy.md:101)
|
| No claim submitted more than 120 days after the expense was incurred
| will be reimbursed in any circumstances.

"In any circumstances" makes E-6.3 absolute, so it sits below `e6_2` in the
chain: it defeats the certification route and admits no exception of its own.
"More than 120 days" is strict, so day 120 is not caught by E-6.3 and day 121
is.

On the encoding above E-6.3 changes no answer that E-6.1 and E-6.2 would not
already give — E-6.2's own upper bound stops at 120 and E-6.1 returns
inadmissible beyond 60 — and it is encoded anyway, at the depth the text puts
it, because the long-stop is the clause a reader needs cited when a 200-day
claim with a glowing certification is refused. An encoding that dropped it would
be relying on E-6.2's bound to do E-6.3's work, and a future amendment widening
E-6.2 would then silently defeat the long-stop.

```catala
scope ClaimAdmissible:
  label e6_3 exception e6_2 definition admissible
    under condition days_elapsed > 120
    consequence equals false
```
